WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.588
FluentValidation is commonly used in C# applications when validation rules become richer than simple attributes.

2
00:00:07.588 --> 00:00:18.042
It lets teams define validators in code, compose reusable rules, express conditional validation, and keep request validation close to application behavior.

3
00:00:18.042 --> 00:00:23.617
This can make complex contracts easier to read than a long set of attributes on a model.

4
00:00:23.617 --> 00:00:31.206
The important benefit is consistency: a request shape should have one clear validator that all entry points can use.

5
00:00:31.206 --> 00:00:38.020
Centralized validators reduce duplication, but they should not become a dumping ground for every rule.

6
00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:43.673
Request validation should answer whether the input is shaped correctly for a use case.

7
00:00:43.673 --> 00:00:49.480
Domain validation should answer whether the operation is allowed by the business model.

8
00:00:49.480 --> 00:01:04.115
Service-layer validation sits between those concerns and is especially important when work can be triggered by controllers, minimal APIs, background jobs, scheduled tasks, message consumers, or internal service calls.

9
00:01:04.115 --> 00:01:08.219
Validation gaps often appear when only controllers validate.

10
00:01:08.219 --> 00:01:12.400
A background job may call the same service with data from a queue.

11
00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:15.265
An integration test may seed data directly.

12
00:01:15.265 --> 00:01:17.898
An internal API may bypass an MVC filter.

13
00:01:17.898 --> 00:01:27.423
If the service layer assumes every caller already validated perfectly, invalid data can reach business-critical logic from a less obvious path.

14
00:01:27.423 --> 00:01:31.449
Shared validators and domain invariants help close that gap.

15
00:01:31.449 --> 00:01:34.624
Maintainability matters as applications grow.

16
00:01:34.624 --> 00:01:42.909
Validators should be named clearly, covered by tests, and organized around use cases rather than broad persistence entities.

17
00:01:42.909 --> 00:01:51.195
Reusable rules are helpful when they represent real shared policy, such as email format, tenant identifiers, or money limits.

18
00:01:51.195 --> 00:01:56.460
They are harmful when abstraction hides context-specific business decisions.

19
00:01:56.460 --> 00:02:03.739
Good validation code is boring in the best way: obvious, explicit, and easy to update when the contract changes.
